"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings indicate a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3) and pT3a stage, with positive surgical margins, but no extraprostatic extension, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, or lymph node metastasis documented. The biopsy showed a Gleason 4+3 diagnosis, which aligns with the resection findings. The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 16.5. The discrepancy between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, though the surgical report is the primary post-operative data. Missing postoperative PSA measurements and follow-up data significantly widen the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory of the PSA. The nodal status was documented as negative, which is reassuring. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 51.41 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 38.56–64.26 months; its coverage has not been validated."
